<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>Why We Buy What We Buy</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 3 · Day 1 — Unit II: Entrepreneurial Trends</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Choral Recall</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>DI Instruction</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Practice / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Logoff</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Opens two new BMG blocks: Value Propositions and Customer Segments. Backwards Design goal: students identify a real trend or unmet need in their own community and articulate why it creates opportunity. Financial literacy touch this week: I. Earning Income; II. Spending.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Two weeks in a row now — entrepreneur takes on...</p>
  <p class="cr">"The risk, keeps the reward or the loss."</p>
  <p class="t">And after Mafia, tell me — opportunity cost means?</p>
  <p class="cr">"What you give up to get something else."</p>
  <p class="t">Good — that one stuck. Today's new word is <span class="k">trend</span>. A pattern in what people want, right now, that's changing.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — I Do / We Do / You Do</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Trends and Customer Segments</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">A <span class="k">trend</span> is a pattern in what people want. A <span class="k">customer segment</span> is a group of people who buy the same thing — but not always for the same reason.</p>
  <p class="t">Here's the part that surprises people: two totally different groups can buy the exact same product. Same item, different reasons. That's still two different customer segments.</p>
  <p class="a">Example: work boots. A rancher buys them for durability on the job. A teenager buys the same brand because they're trending as a style right now. Same boots — two segments, two reasons.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Sorting Reasons</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">I'll name a product. You tell me two different reasons two different kinds of people might buy it.</p>
  <p class="a">Examples to use: a pickup truck, a phone case, cowboy boots, a cooler. Push for genuinely different reasons, not just "because they like it" twice.</p>
  <p class="t">Notice — the product didn't change. The <span class="k">reason</span> changed. That's what a business has to understand about its customers.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  Hear/Say into Think/Say — the sorting exercise is intentionally quick and social before the slower, more personal trend-spotting work begins later this week.
</div>

<div class="note iefa">
  <span class="label">IEFA Connection</span>
  Trends within the Blackfeet Nation specifically — youth-run businesses at powwows, seasonal tourism, and language-revitalization products (things like Blackfoot-language flashcards, apparel, or learning tools) are all real, current, local examples of trends creating opportunity. Good material for tomorrow's deeper dive.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">In your workbook, pick one product and write two different reasons two different people might buy it.</p>
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  Watch for students who write two reasons that are actually the same idea reworded ("they like it" / "they think it's cool"). Push for something concretely different — practical need vs. style, for instance.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Practice — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Independent Practice</h3>
<p>Students finish their two-reasons write-up, then brainstorm: name one thing that's popular right now among people their age, locally — this seeds Day 2's deeper customer segment work.</p>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Who I Help" (Value Proposition, Simplified)</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">Different people need help in different ways. If you helped an elder, what would you help with?</p>
    <p class="s">[names something — carrying, reading, cooking]</p>
    <p class="t">If you helped a little kid, would it be the same thing?</p>
    <p class="s">[usually no]</p>
    <p class="t">Same idea — different people need different things. That's what businesses have to think about too.</p>
    <p class="a">Use picture cards of different people (elder, young child, peer) — students match a simple "help" picture card to each person, reinforcing that the same helper can serve different people differently.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">One sentence: name one product, and two different reasons two different people might buy it.</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>You Already Read People</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. Understanding that different people want different things for different reasons isn't a business school skill you're learning from nothing — it's something you already do every day with the people around you. Tomorrow we go deeper.</p>
</div>

</div>
