</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Direct build on the Backwards Design goal: students see how even people unlike each other can land in the same — or a different — customer segment for the exact same product. Financial literacy touch: II. Spending.
</div>

<div class="note iefa">
  <span class="label">IEFA Connection</span>
  Today's case study uses beadwork — a real product made and sold within the Blackfeet Nation — to show two genuinely different customer segments buying the same kind of item for very different reasons.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Yesterday — customer segment means?</p>
  <p class="cr">"A group of people who buy the same thing."</p>
  <p class="t">And two segments can buy the exact same product for...</p>
  <p class="cr">"Different reasons."</p>
  <p class="t">Today, one real example — beadwork.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — Live Case Study</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Two Buyers, One Product</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">A beadwork artist sells the same style of earrings to two very different customers. Customer one — a community member buying them to wear at a powwow, connected to identity and tradition. Customer two — a tourist passing through, buying them as a souvenir to take home.</p>
  <p class="a">Ask: same earrings — what's different about why each person is buying them?</p>
  <p class="t">Same <span class="k">product</span>. Two different <span class="k">customer segments</span>. Community member and tourist — neither one is "more real" as a customer. They just want different things from the same purchase.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — What Changes, What Doesn't</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Does the artist need to make two different products?</p>
  <p class="s">[no]</p>
  <p class="t">What might change about how they talk about the earrings to each customer, even if the product is the same?</p>
  <p class="a">Take answers — expect: emphasizing meaning/tradition to the community member, emphasizing uniqueness/craftsmanship to the tourist. This is the seed of Value Proposition, coming later in the unit — don't over-teach it yet, just plant it.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  See/Say — a concrete two-buyer example does more work here than an abstract definition. Keep pointing back to "same product, different reason" as the throughline.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Pick your own local example — something sold here that different kinds of people buy for different reasons. Fill in the comparison chart.</p>
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  If a student picks two "reasons" that are really the same reason worded differently, this is the same gap from Day 1 — circle back with a direct question: "would that person buy it if the other reason didn't exist?"
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Compare Chart — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Comparison Chart</h3>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col">
    <h4>Buyer 1</h4>
    Who: ____________<br>
    Why they're buying it: ____________
  </div>
  <div class="col">
    <h4>Buyer 2</h4>
    Who: ____________<br>
    Why they're buying it: ____________
  </div>
</div>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Same Thing, Different Reason"</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">Two people can buy the exact same thing for different reasons. Look — here's a picture of earrings. One person wears them because they mean something to their family. Another person buys them because they think they're pretty and want to remember their trip.</p>
    <p class="a">Use simple picture cards — same earrings image twice, paired with a "home/family" icon and a "traveler/suitcase" icon.</p>
    <p class="t">Same earrings. Two different reasons. Both are okay.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">One sentence: name a product, and two different reasons two different people might buy it — from a local example, not one I gave you.</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent per student.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>What You Sell and Why It Matters Aren't the Same Thing</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. The same thing you make or offer can matter to different people in completely different ways — and both are valid. Tomorrow we slow down and get fast and accurate with everything we've learned so far.</p>
</div>

