  Precision Teaching day — fluency (accuracy + speed) on Week 3 vocabulary: trend, customer segment, target audience, opportunity. Anchored in real, current, local trend examples so it isn't abstract. Financial literacy touch: IV. Investing; VI. Managing Risk — spotting a trend early is itself a risk/reward decision.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Quick fire. Trend means?</p>
  <p class="cr">"A pattern in what people want."</p>
  <p class="t">Customer segment means?</p>
  <p class="cr">"A group of people who buy the same thing."</p>
  <p class="t">Today, no new ideas — just getting fast and accurate.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · Local Trends Mini-Lesson + Fluency Rounds</h2>

<div class="note iefa">
  <span class="label">IEFA Connection — Real, Current, Local Trends</span>
  Youth-run businesses at powwows, seasonal tourism tied to the reservation's land and events, and language-revitalization products (Blackfoot-language learning materials, apparel, and tools) are all real, current trends within the Blackfeet Nation — genuine opportunity, not hypothetical examples. If you know of specific current student or community ventures fitting this pattern, they'll land far better here than the general categories alone.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — Trends You Can Actually See</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">A trend isn't something that only happens in big cities or on the internet. Right now, in this community, there are real trends creating real opportunity — youth selling things at powwows, businesses built around seasonal tourism, and a growing interest in Blackfoot-language products — flashcards, apparel, learning tools.</p>
  <p class="t">Every one of those started because someone noticed a pattern in what people wanted, and acted on it.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">Precision Teaching — Three-Round Fluency Structure</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="a"><span class="k">Round 1 — With Support:</span> Word wall visible (trend, customer segment, target audience, opportunity, value proposition). Call a term, students respond chorally, checking wall as needed.</p>
  <p class="a"><span class="k">Round 2 — Without Support:</span> Cover the word wall. Timed — 60 seconds. Call terms rapid-fire, individual students respond, log speed and accuracy.</p>
</div>

<div class="fluency-table">
  <tr><th>Round</th><th>Support</th><th>Timing</th><th>Response Mode</th></tr>
  <tr><td>1</td><td>Word wall visible</td><td>Untimed</td><td>Choral</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2</td><td>None</td><td>60 sec, timed</td><td>Individual, cold-call</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3</td><td>None</td><td>Untimed</td><td>Peer partner quiz</td></tr>
</div>

<div class="note adhd">
  <span class="label">ADHD Observation Tip</span>
  Same routine as the previous two fluency days — the consistency itself is doing work here. By Week 3, the "hands down, wait for your name" cue should be familiar enough that less redirection is needed. If it's still not landing, that's worth noting for a possible individual accommodation.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Round 3 — Peer Identification / SPED Pull-Aside</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Round 3</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Partner up, quiz each other on all five terms. If your partner's stuck, ask a question that helps them find the answer.</p>
  <p class="a">Circulate and log Not Yet/Developing/Fluent per student based on Rounds 2 and 3 combined.</p>
</div>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "What's Popular Right Now?"</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level. Untimed, repeated practice reps.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">A trend is something a lot of people want right now. What's something you've seen a lot of people wearing, using, or talking about lately?</p>
    <p class="s">[names something]</p>
    <p class="t">That's a trend. If someone made or sold that thing, people would probably want to buy it.</p>
    <p class="a">Use picture cards of simple, recognizable local trend examples (powwow regalia accessories, beaded jewelry, seasonal items) — students point to or sort which ones they've seen recently.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Fast round, no writing: I say a term, you say the definition.</p>
</div>

