<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Launches the Week 3 end-of-week artifact — "A need I see in my community," one paragraph, which becomes the raw material Week 4 uses to generate and validate an actual idea. Financial literacy touch: VI. Managing Risk — spotting an unmet need is a bet, not a guarantee.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Choral Recall</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">This week — trend means?</p>
  <p class="cr">"A pattern in what people want."</p>
  <p class="t">Today we stop talking about other people's trends. You go find your own.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — Finding an Unmet Need</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — What Makes a Need "Unmet"</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Not every need is a business opportunity — only the ones that aren't already being solved well. That's called an <span class="k">unmet need</span>. Something people deal with, want fixed, or wish existed — and right now, nobody local is doing it, or nobody's doing it well.</p>
  <p class="a">Model your own example: something small and real, e.g., "there's nowhere close by to get X fixed quickly" or "there's no easy way for elders to get Y."</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Brainstorming Categories</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Let's brainstorm categories where unmet needs tend to hide — call some out with me.</p>
  <p class="a">Write on board without judgment: something that takes too long right now, something too expensive, something people have to travel too far for, something nobody local offers, something only certain people can access.</p>
  <p class="t">You don't need a business idea yet. You just need to notice a real problem.</p>
</div>

<div class="note channel">
  <span class="label">Learning Channel</span>
  Hear/Say (category brainstorm) into Think/Write (personal reflection) — same structure as Week 1 Day 4's Key Activities brainstorm, giving students a shared starting list before they write something personal.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Guided Practice</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Start your paragraph. What's a need you actually see — in your family, your school, or this community? Be specific. "People need more stuff" isn't specific. "There's no place close by to get a bike fixed" is specific.</p>
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  Watch for vague, generic answers ("people need more jobs," "people need more money") — these are true but not usable. Push with "where exactly have you seen this? who exactly deals with this?" to get something concrete enough to build on in Week 4.
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Research Time — Two Tracks Running Concurrently</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Drafting the Artifact</h3>
<div class="workbook-box">
  <span class="wb-label">Draft: A Need I See in My Community</span>
  One paragraph, specific, real. This is rough-draft language — Day 5 finalizes it into the portfolio piece.
</div>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — "Something That Bugs Me"</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">Is there something that's hard to find, or hard to get done, near where you live?</p>
    <p class="s">[names something]</p>
    <p class="t">That's a real need. That could be a business idea someday.</p>
    <p class="a">Use a simple picture-based prompt sheet with categories (something hard to find, something that takes too long, something too far away) — student picks or points to the category that fits, then names the specific thing.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check</h2>
<div class="script">
