<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Entrepreneurship · Browning High School</div>
  <h1>Putting the Need Into Words</h1>
  <div class="sub">Week 3 · Day 5 — Portfolio Piece, Preview of Week 4</div>
</header>

<div class="timestrip">
  <div class="ts-login">0:00–8:00<br>Login + Typing</div>
  <div class="ts-review">8:00–11:00<br>Week 3 Recap</div>
  <div class="ts-di">11:00–29:00<br>Finalize Paragraph</div>
  <div class="ts-practice">29:00–40:00<br>Share Out / SPED Pull-Aside</div>
  <div class="ts-exit">40:00–45:00<br>Exit + Preview Week 4</div>
</div>

<div class="wrap">

<div class="note goal">
  <span class="label">Unit Goal Connection</span>
  Finalizes the Week 3 end-of-week artifact — "A need I see in my community" — for the running Week 1–9 workbook section. This becomes the starting point for Week 4's idea generation and validation.
</div>

<h2 class="section">0:00–8:00 · Login &amp; Typing Practice</h2>
<p>Standard routine.</p>

<h2 class="section">8:00–11:00 · Week 3 Recap</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">This week: trend means?</p>
  <p class="cr">"A pattern in what people want."</p>
  <p class="t">Customer segment means?</p>
  <p class="cr">"A group of people who buy the same thing."</p>
  <p class="t">And an unmet need is a problem that's...</p>
  <p class="cr">"Not already being solved well."</p>
  <p class="t">Today you finish naming yours.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">11:00–29:00 · DI Instruction — Finalizing the Paragraph</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">I Do — What Makes a Need Specific Enough</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Yesterday I said "people need more stuff" isn't specific enough. Watch what changes when I make mine specific.</p>
  <p class="a">Model turning a vague draft into a specific one: "People need better transportation" becomes "There's no easy way for elders in this community to get a ride to appointments when family isn't available." Point out exactly what made it specific — who, what, and why it's not already solved.</p>
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">We Do — Peer Feedback Pass</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Trade workbooks. Read your partner's paragraph. Ask them: who exactly deals with this, and why isn't it already solved?</p>
  <p class="a">Same peer error-correction structure as Week 1 Day 5 — the partner asks a question, doesn't fill in the answer.</p>
</div>

<div class="note comprehension">
  <span class="label">Comprehension Tip</span>
  This is the same specificity gap as Day 4 — watch for paragraphs that stayed vague overnight. The peer question ("who exactly?") is designed to catch it a second time if it wasn't caught the first.
</div>

<h3 class="subsection">You Do — Final Write</h3>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Revise your paragraph using your partner's feedback. This is the version that goes in your portfolio and becomes the starting point for next week.</p>
</div>

<h2 class="section">29:00–40:00 · Voluntary Share-Out / Two Tracks</h2>

<h3 class="subsection">Main Class — Optional Share</h3>
<p>Ask for 3–4 volunteers to read their paragraph aloud. Not mandatory.</p>

<div class="sped-block">
  <h3 class="subsection">SPED Pull-Aside — Finishing the Week</h3>
  <p style="margin-top:0;"><strong>Format:</strong> One small group, teacher-led, 2nd-grade reading level.</p>
  <div class="sped-script">
    <p class="t">This week you found something that bugs you or someone you know. Let's put it in one sentence. Near me, it is hard to [problem].</p>
    <p class="a">Help each student assemble their own sentence orally, then support them writing or dictating it into the workbook.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section">40:00–45:00 · Exit Check + Preview Week 4</h2>
<div class="script">
  <p class="t">Before you log off — next week we take the need you just named and start turning it into an actual idea. We'll test it a little too, so we're not just guessing. Bring your paragraph back Monday — you'll need it.</p>
  <p class="a">Not Yet / Developing / Fluent — final Week 3 mark, based on the finished artifact's specificity.</p>
</div>

<table class="assess">
  <tr><th>Student</th><th>Not Yet</th><th>Developing</th><th>Fluent</th></tr>
  <tr><td colspan="4" style="color:#888;">(roster rows — printed version)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="identity">
  <h3>What You Named Is Real</h3>
  <p><span class="cap">Important names are capitalized</span> — and you are an important person. What you wrote this week isn't a made-up school example — it's a real problem you actually noticed. That's the hardest part of starting a business, and you already did it. It stays in your workbook, and next week it becomes the seed of your own idea.</p>
</div>

</div>
